  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDry weather is expected to continue through Tuesday night as \nhigh pressure moves east across the Upper Ohio Valley region. \nLowering humidity levels are also expected with NW flow. Warm \ndaytime temperatures, and overnight lows in the 50s, are \nexpected through Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModel ensembles indicate a broad trough will begin to approach \nfrom the northern Plains/Upper Midwest region Tuesday night into\nWednesday. At the same time, a closed SE CONUS low is progged \nto phase with the trough as it approaches the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion. \n\nIncreasing moisture and lift in southwest flow should return \nscattered shower and thunderstorm chances to the region on \nWednesday. Daily shower and thunderstorm chances (peaking during\nafternoon/evening heating) continue through at least the end of\nthe work week, and possibly into the weekend, as a series of \nembedded shortwaves traverse the Upper Ohio Valley region.\nForecast soundings suggest each afternoon will be characterized\nby a relatively unchanging environment with 1500-2000 J/kg \nSBCAPE, ~600 J/kg DCAPE, and PWATs approaching 2.0 inches. These\ningredients could end up supporting a low threat for strong wind\ngusts and localized flash flooding due to heavy rainfall in\nthunderstorms. Will continue to monitor the potential for these\nhazards, but as of now there is still too much uncertainty to \nnail down any specifics.",
